

--- 
The central theoretical progression
Physics
Indeterminacy, symmetry, constraints, and persistent physical structures
Assembly theory
Historical construction, complex structures, and reproductive frequency
Biology
Energy capture, autopoiesis, and recursive environmental transformation
Technology 
More capable energy-extracting systems and hierarchical productive organization
Historical materialism
Social relationships as historically assembled conditions of production
Cybernetics
Feedback, self-maintenance, hierarchy, and social reproduction
Artificial intelligence
Internal models, optimization, and potential recursive improvement of productive capacity
Synthesis
Can one material process connect these different histories?
---
Three things I'd emphasize when writing
First, energy extraction remains the engine of the argument. Every chapter should ask how energy is accessed, directed, and transformed into new productive capacity. The book's strongest proposal is that major transitions in complexity are associated with assemblages acquiring more powerful energetic capabilities.
Second, autopoiesis and cybernetics should make historical materialism more explanatory. Rather than simply describing capitalism as a system of exploitation, Chapters 9 and 10 should examine how social relationships recursively reproduce themselves, how their persistence creates stability, and why competing material interests and external shocks can force transformation.
Third, the distinction between a system modeling its environment and modeling itself should be introduced in Chapter 11. That provides a bridge from biological self-regulation to human consciousness, collective intelligence, and AI. Functional self-models are not exclusive to humans, and whether they entail subjective awareness remains unsettled; that question could become one of the chapter's intellectual tensions.
The book would now have a unified narrative: physical uncertainty and constraints give rise to structures; structures create the conditions for more structures; energy extraction amplifies their reproduction; cybernetic feedback produces self-maintaining hierarchies; human society reproduces material relationships; and AI potentially accelerates the assembly of the systems that sustain it. 
Chapter 12 returns to the original question of whether this is a set of related mechanisms operating at different scales, or evidence of a deeper material principle governing the history of reality.


