…
According to the Maximum Entropy Production Principle (MEPP) and physicist Eric Chaisson's work on cosmic evolution, complex systems emerge specifically because they are better at draining energy gradients than simple, inanimate matter
The system that was out of equilibrium—and remained "locked" for 290,000 years—is Earth’s biogeochemical carbon and energy reservoir, which only became accessible once the climate stabilized and humans crossed a critical information threshold.
thermodynamic systems cannot maximize entropy production if kinetic barriers or environmental instability block the energy flow.
an out-of-equilibrium system will sit idle until a pathway opens to drain its energy. Two major barriers prevented humans from building a high-entropy-producing civilization earlier:
Climate Instability (The Pleistocene): For almost all of human existence, Earth was locked in the chaotic fluctuations of the Ice Age. Rapid, extreme swings in temperature and rainfall prevented stable agriculture. Without reliable crop surpluses, human populations remained small, dispersed hunter-gatherers limited to basic metabolic energy (roughly 100 watts per person).
High Activation Energy Barriers: Vast stores of concentrated energy—like coal, oil, and mineral ores—were trapped underground. Accessing them required complex tools, metallurgy, and scientific knowledge that could not develop without surplus energy and dense populations.
The Holocene Climate Optimum: Earth entered an unusually warm, stable interglacial period. This stability allowed humans to domesticate plants and animals (the Agricultural Revolution).
Information as a Catalyst: Agriculture tapped into the Sun-Earth-Soil energy gradient much more efficiently than foraging. This created an energy surplus, allowing human populations to grow and specialize. In thermodynamic terms, human knowledge and technology act as a catalyst—lowering the activation energy required to tap into ever-deeper energy gradients (first biomass, then wind/water, then fossil fuels, and now nuclear and solar).
Before 10,000 years ago: The biosphere had reached a relatively steady state of entropy production (plants absorbing sunlight, animals eating plants).
Today: Human technology has cracked open the geological carbon vault (fossil fuels) and atomic nuclei (uranium), creating a brand-new, massive thermodynamic disequilibrium between concentrated terrestrial fuels and Earth's atmosphere/space. Civilization is the physical structure rapidly draining that newly unlocked reservoir toward equilibrium.


